---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const items = [
  {
    "n": "01",
    "t": "Start with people and feasibility",
    "b": "Gather a working group, then explore land without rushing into an irreversible purchase. Site opportunities sometimes arrive early; use professional due diligence, conditional agreements and a clear limit on what the group can risk."
  },
  {
    "n": "02",
    "t": "Make participation possible",
    "b": "Begin with enough committed people to share the work. There is no magic household count. Budget facilitation, translated information, childcare and meeting access where needed. A project should not select its future residents only by who has spare time."
  },
  {
    "n": "03",
    "t": "Write the awkward bits while everyone is friendly",
    "b": "Record entry costs, exit rights, decision rules, conflict resolution, care work and quiet space. Include arrears, job loss, illness and death—not just the cheerful dinner rota. Have the legal documents reviewed; a friendly charter cannot override residents’ rights."
  },
  {
    "n": "04",
    "t": "Choose the ownership with help",
    "b": "Compare rental, cooperative, limited-equity and land-trust arrangements against the group’s aims. A CLT can hold land for community purposes, but affordability depends on the full financing, restrictions and stewardship. Talk with an existing local provider before creating a new organization."
  },
  {
    "n": "05",
    "t": "Pay for the development work",
    "b": "Resident-led development can align choices with residents. It also carries professional fees, financing, insurance, contingency and real risk. Cost every role, including project management. Removing a speculative margin does not remove the work or guarantee a saving."
  },
  {
    "n": "06",
    "t": "Compare new build and reuse",
    "b": "New construction can fit a new brief; reuse may retain buildings and existing communities. Commission surveys for structure, hazards, services and accessibility. Model renovation disruption and tenant protections. Neither route is automatically faster, cheaper or kinder."
  },
  {
    "n": "07",
    "t": "Bring safety and access to the first sketch",
    "b": "Have qualified designers and code specialists evaluate exits, rescue access, sprinklers, accessible routes, lifts and construction type together. An open gallery is not a shortcut around safety. Check adopted rules locally; a pending bill or study is not permission to build."
  },
  {
    "n": "08",
    "t": "Give every shared room a job",
    "b": "For the café, laundry or workshop, name the user, operator, hours, cleaning arrangements and funding. Model vacancies and repairs. A flexible residential or community use may be more useful than a storefront with no viable tenant."
  },
  {
    "n": "09",
    "t": "Publish what the next group can use",
    "b": "Share the brief, assumptions, ranges, decisions and lessons—with consent and private financial details removed. A dated feasibility note is more useful than a confident number with no source. Update it when the site or financing changes."
  }
];
---

<Base title="How — The Courtyard Block" current="how">
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-[920px] px-6 pt-20 sm:px-12">
    <p class="mb-4 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-terracotta">Practically</p>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(40px,5.5vw,68px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.015em]">
      So, who takes the bins out?
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[65ch] text-[22px] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80">
      The useful questions behind a lovely building. An iterative starting sequence for a group—not a guaranteed development schedule.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-[920px] px-6 sm:px-12">
    {
      items.map((i) => (
        <article class="grid grid-cols-[64px_1fr] gap-6 border-t border-ink/15 py-7.5">
          <p class="pt-1 font-sans text-sm text-terracotta">{i.n}</p>
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-[26px] font-medium">{i.t}</h2>
            <p class="mt-2.5 max-w-[65ch] text-lg leading-[1.6] text-ink/85">{i.b}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto mt-20 max-w-[920px] px-6 sm:px-12">
    <div class="bg-ink px-12 py-13 text-paper">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#e08b6d]">Join</p>
      <p class="mt-3.5 text-[clamp(24px,3vw,34px)] font-medium leading-[1.3] text-paper">
        This site is the draft. If you would live in this building, help write it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.6] text-paper/80">
        Contribute ideas, corrections, and case studies on
        <a href="https://github.com/crs48/courtyard-block-site" class="text-[#e08b6d] hover:text-[#e08b6d]"
          >GitHub</a
        > — open an issue or a pull request — or propose a parcel you walk past every day.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[920px] px-6 sm:px-12"><h2 class="text-3xl">Keep these on the meeting table</h2><p class="mt-4 text-xl leading-relaxed">For land stewardship, start with <a href="https://groundedsolutions.org/strengthening-neighborhoods/community-land-trusts/">Grounded Solutions’ CLT overview</a>. For the distinction between zoning and building permission, use <a href="https://berkeleyca.gov/construction-development/permits-design-parameters/permit-types/zoning-permits">Berkeley’s permit guide</a>. For accessible routes and common spaces in covered multifamily housing, read <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/dfiles/FHEO/documents/TECHNICAL_OVERVIEW.pdf">HUD’s design requirements (PDF)</a>. These explain parts of the work; the right local professionals must assess a specific project.</p><p class="research-note mt-6">A useful first meeting output: one page naming who the housing is for, what they can afford, what they want to share, and which questions need paid expertise. The floor plan can wait until dessert.</p></section>
</Base>
